     2	name: obsidian-vault
     3	description: Lease an Obsidian test vault to the current worktree so its dev watcher and CDP runs never collide with another branch's. Use whenever starting the apps/obsidian dev watcher, building the plugin into a vault, choosing or switching which vault a branch uses, asking "which vault is free?", or when a build seems to have been overwritten by another worktree.
     4	---
     5	
     6	# Obsidian vault leases
     7	
     8	One script, `~/.claude/skills/obsidian-vault/scripts/vault.mjs`, owns which worktree builds into which vault. Pass `--worktree <abs path>` on every call: the Bash cwd resets to the main repo between calls.
     9	
    10	```sh
    11	V=~/.claude/skills/obsidian-vault/scripts/vault.mjs
    12	node $V status                          # every vault: free | idle | live, holder, watcher, CDP run
    13	node $V dev --worktree <wt>             # claim + start the watcher (run_in_background)
    14	node $V claim --worktree <wt>           # claim without starting anything
    15	node $V claim --worktree <wt> --take <vault>   # only after the user approved it
    16	node $V release --worktree <wt>         # stop this worktree's watcher, free its vault
    17	node $V path --worktree <wt>            # print the leased plugin dir
    18	```
    19	
    20	## Pools
    21	
    22	The registry at `~/.claude/state/obsidian-vaults.json` holds the pool and the leases. Edit its `vaults` array to add or remove vaults.
    23	
    24	- `--db local` (default): dgDevVault, testVault, testVault2, Obsidian Vault. Normal dev work.
    25	- `--db production`: testVault123. Use only when Trang asks to test against the prod database.
    26	
    27	## The worktree's `.env` follows the lease
    28	
    29	The lease is the source of truth, and `<worktree>/apps/obsidian/.env` mirrors it. Every claim writes `OBSIDIAN_PLUGIN_PATH` and `SUPABASE_USE_DB` there and keeps the other lines. A plain `pnpm dev`, `vault.mjs dev` and `dg-obsidian-cdp-verify` therefore all build into the same vault.
    30	
    31	- A takeover clears the losing worktree's two keys, but only if they still point at that vault, so its next `pnpm dev` doesn't mirror anywhere.
    32	- `release` clears them the same way.
    33	- Don't hand-edit these keys. Claim or `--take` instead, or the `.env` and the registry disagree.
    34	- A running watcher read `.env` at startup. If `claim` prints `restartWatcher`, restart `pnpm dev`.
    35	
    36	## States
    37	
    38	- **free**: no lease, nothing running. `claim` takes it silently.
    39	- **idle**: leased to a worktree, but no watcher or CDP run. The session may still be alive and planning.
    40	- **live**: an apps/obsidian `dev.ts` watcher mirrors into it (leased or not, found via `ps` + `lsof` cwd), or a `dg-obsidian-cdp-verify` run holds its lock.
    41	
    42	A worktree re-claiming gets its own vault back. A lease frees itself only when its worktree directory is gone, or on `release`.
    43	
    44	## When `claim` or `dev` exits 2
    45	
    46	No free vault is left in that pool. **Never pick one yourself.**
    47	
    48	1. Read the printed `candidates` (vault, state, holder branch, since, cdpRun).
    49	2. Call `mcp__ccd_session_mgmt__list_sessions` (load it with ToolSearch) and note whether a session is active on each holder branch.
    50	3. Ask Trang with AskUserQuestion: one option per vault, labelled with its state, its holder and whether that holder's session is active.
    51	4. Only on a yes, rerun with `--take <vault>`. On a live vault, this SIGTERMs the holder's watcher. A vault with a running CDP run can't be taken; wait for it.
    52	
    53	## After claiming
    54	
    55	- Open the vault in Obsidian with the printed `obsidian://open?path=` URL, and reload the plugin.
    56	- `dg-obsidian-cdp-verify` uses the lease automatically when no `--slot` is given.
